  "title": "Building Cooperation Through Connection",
  "content": "Children are more cooperative when they feel connected to their caregivers. Communication rooted in connection—rather than control—creates a sense of teamwork. When parents approach instructions with warmth, eye contact, and empathy, children are more willing to follow through.\n\nThis lesson explores how connection acts as the foundation for effective communication. Parents learn how to use gentle touch, shared attention, and positive phrasing to increase cooperation. Instead of demanding compliance, parents learn to invite cooperation.\n\nConnection‑based communication reduces power struggles and strengthens the parent‑child relationship over time.",
  "activities": [
    "Before giving an instruction, connect with your child using eye contact or a gentle touch. Then give the instruction calmly.",
    "Use a positive phrasing technique today: instead of saying 'Don’t run,' try 'Walk next to me.'"
